              "text": "(1) More than half of the world's population is thought to speak at least two languages. In many countries, such as Mongolia, children grow up hearing one language at home and learning others at school. For a long time, however, people believed that learning two languages at once might confuse young children.\n\n(2) Today, most researchers disagree. Young children are very good at keeping languages apart. They may mix words from both languages for a while, but this is a normal stage, not a sign of confusion, and it usually disappears as their vocabulary grows.\n\n(3) Some scientists believe that speaking two languages is also good exercise for the brain. When bilingual people speak, both languages are active in their minds, so they must constantly choose one and ignore the other. This may train the parts of the brain that control attention.\n\n(4) A number of studies have even suggested that bilingual people tend to show signs of memory loss in old age several years later than people who speak only one language. Not all studies agree, however, and researchers are still trying to understand why the results differ.\n\n(5) Of course, there are challenges. Bilingual children may know fewer words in each separate language than children who speak only one, although their total vocabulary is often similar. Adults who learn a language later in life usually keep an accent, even if they speak very well.\n\n(6) Whatever the scientific debate, most people agree that knowing another language opens doors. It allows us to travel, to read, to make friends and to see the world from a different point of view. In that sense, the greatest benefit of a second language may not be in the brain at all, but in the people it helps us meet.",
